"A sequence diagram orders messages among participants for a bounded interaction scenario.\n\nUse when: Explain one request across several services or objects. Review alternatives, retries, and transaction boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: A portfolio inventory or all possible lifecycle states is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Lifelines denote participants with time proceeding downward. Arrow styles distinguish calls, asynchronous messages, and replies under a legend. Combined fragments encode alternatives, loops, or parallel behavior.\n\nBuild: Choose one scenario with a named trigger and outcome. Order participants to minimize long crossing messages. Add messages in causal order using concise verb phrases. Place guards and fragment labels in reserved header strips; route notes outside message corridors.\n\nCheck: Messages arrive after they are sent and target valid participants. Synchronous waiting is not implied for asynchronous delivery. Timeouts and retries identify their conditions and correlation. No message line crosses a lifeline title or fragment label.\n\nPitfalls: Treating vertical spacing as measured duration without a time scale. Combining every scenario into one unreadable alternative fragment.\n\nExample: Checkout asks Inventory to hold items, requests Payment authorization, and confirms the order after both succeed.\n\nAlternatives: Communication diagram, Transaction schedule diagram."
